Yes. If you want to do this **like a real product/design project**, don't open Figma immediately.

Follow this order:

## Step 1 — Understand the current product

First spend **30–60 minutes** only understanding Tutedude.

Check:

* Home page
* Course page
* All Access page
* Combo Pack page
* Reviews page
* FAQs

Your goal is to answer:

> **“If I am a student who has never heard of Tutedude, what do I understand after 30 seconds?”**

The brief specifically asks you to think like a stranger, not like an existing student. 

---

# Step 2 — Identify the real problems

Create a simple document like this:

| Problem                      | Why it is a problem              | Design solution                     |
| ---------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Don't understand Tutedude    | User sees another course website | Explain the product clearly in hero |
| Refund is confusing          | User doesn't trust it            | Create a simple refund explanation  |
| Too many courses/cards       | Important message gets lost      | Improve hierarchy                   |
| Claims don't feel believable | User needs proof                 | Add evidence/reviews/results        |
| Weak CTA                     | User doesn't know what to do     | Use action-based CTA                |

The brief itself gives you the three biggest problems:

**What is Tutedude? → Refund doesn't land → Page doesn't sell.** 

---

# Step 3 — Define your brand direction

Before Figma, decide:

### Brand feeling

For example:

> **Confident + youthful + premium + trustworthy + student-focused**

Then define:

* Colors
* Typography
* Heading style
* Body text
* Button style
* Card style
* Border radius
* Shadows
* Icons
* Illustration/image style
* Spacing system

Make a small **Brand System** page in Figma.

The brief specifically says the logo is the only fixed element; everything else is your decision. 

---

# Step 4 — Design the user journey

This is VERY important.

Don't think:

> "What sections should I put?"

Think:

> **"What question does the student have at every scroll?"**

For example:

### Scroll 1

**What is Tutedude?**

↓

### Scroll 2

**Why should I trust this?**

↓

### Scroll 3

**What exactly do I get?**

↓

### Scroll 4

**How does the ₹699 → 100% refund work?**

↓

### Scroll 5

**Is this actually useful?**

↓

### Scroll 6

**Can I see proof?**

↓

### Scroll 7

**Which course should I choose?**

↓

### Scroll 8

**What if I still have doubts?**

↓

### Final

**Take action**

That becomes your actual UX architecture.

---

# Step 5 — Build the Home Page wireframe

Do **black/white wireframe first**.

Don't waste time on colors.

Something like:

```text
HEADER

HERO
"What is Tutedude?"
100% Refund
CTA

PROOF / STATS

HOW TUTEDUDE WORKS

WHAT YOU GET

COURSES

COMBO PACKS

100% REFUND EXPLAINED

MENTORSHIP / PROJECTS / COMMUNITY

WHY TUTEDUDE

PROOF / STUDENT RESULTS

REVIEWS

FAQ

FINAL CTA

FOOTER
```

This is only your **structure**, not the final sections.

---

# Step 6 — Solve the refund BEFORE polishing anything

This is the biggest part of the assignment.

The brief literally says:

> **“Solve the refund explanation before anything else.”** 

Make the user understand:

```text
PAY ₹699
     ↓
LEARN THE COURSE
     ↓
COMPLETE WITHIN 3 MONTHS
     ↓
MEET REFUND CONDITIONS
     ↓
GET 100% MONEY BACK
```

Then explain the conditions clearly.

No complicated marketing language.

The user should not have to ask:

> "Wait, is this actually free?"

---

# Step 7 — Design the Course Page

Now create **one reusable template**.

Think:

```text
COURSE HERO

What is this course?

WHO IS THIS FOR?

WHAT YOU WILL LEARN

CURRICULUM

PROJECTS

MENTORSHIP

COMMUNITY

CERTIFICATE

REFUND EXPLANATION

PROOF / REVIEWS

PRICE

FAQ

FINAL CTA
```

Don't design only the UI/UX course.

Use placeholders like:

```text
{Course Name}
{Course Description}
{Duration}
{Projects}
{Curriculum}
{Price}
```

Because the brief requires the course page to work for **every course**. 

---

# Step 8 — Write the copy

Do this **before final visual design**.

For every section ask:

> **What is the ONE thing I want the user to understand here?**

The brief specifically says:

> **One thing per section.** 

Example:

### Instead of

> Level Up Your Career With Expert Mentorship & Internships...

Try a clearer structure:

**Learn. Build. Get Mentored. Get Your Money Back.**

Then supporting text explains exactly how it works.

Don't try to sound clever.

---

# Step 9 — Create mobile first

Start with:

### 390 × 844

Design the **entire homepage** on mobile.

Then:

### 1440 × 900

adapt it to desktop.

Do NOT make desktop first and squeeze it into mobile later.

The brief explicitly requires mobile-first. 

---

# Step 10 — Add visual design

Now apply:

* Brand colors
* Typography
* Images
* Icons
* Cards
* Gradients if needed
* Animations/interactions
* Hover states
* Sticky CTA where useful
* Responsive behavior

But keep asking:

> **Does this help the student understand something?**

If not, remove it.

---

# Step 11 — Prototype the real journey

Connect:

```text
HOME
 ↓
Course
 ↓
Course Details
 ↓
CTA
```

Also test:

```text
Home → Course
Home → Combo
Home → All Access
Home → FAQ
```

The submission requires a working prototype connecting the Home Page and Course Landing Page. 

---

# Step 12 — Test with a real person

This is one of the most important steps.

Take your design and show it to someone who **doesn't know Tutedude**.

Don't explain anything.

Ask them:

### Question 1

**"What does Tutedude do?"**

### Question 2

**"What do you get?"**

### Question 3

**"How does the 100% refund work?"**

### Question 4

**"What would you click?"**

If they cannot answer quickly:

**your UX isn't clear enough.**

The brief explicitly recommends this test. 

---

# Step 13 — Polish

Only now check:

* Typography
* Alignment
* Spacing
* Consistency
* Mobile responsiveness
* Button hierarchy
* Image quality
* Card consistency
* Empty states
* Existing-user state
* Discount + combo + refund pricing
* Closed/sold-out course state

These edge cases are explicitly mentioned in the brief. 

---

# Step 14 — Make your Figma file professional

Structure it like:

```text
00 — Cover

01 — Brand System

02 — Research / Problems

03 — User Journey

04 — Home — Mobile

05 — Home — Desktop

06 — Course — Mobile

07 — Course — Desktop

08 — Components

09 — Prototype

10 — Design Decisions
```

Keep:

* Color styles
* Text styles
* Components
* Variants
* Proper frame names

inside Figma, as requested. 

